WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.375
LARRY ARNN: And
as it gets close,

00:00:01.375 --> 00:00:02.610
he gives the orders to fire.

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:04.565
And Washington was
shrouded in smoke.

00:00:04.565 --> 00:00:06.690
And the smoke was cleared,
and Washington was still

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.400
walking toward the
British on his horse.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:15.230
[GENTLE MUSIC PLAYS]

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:17.420
What kind of virtue is courage?

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:18.860
STUDENT: Moral.

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:20.960
LARRY ARNN: Doing, right,
moral doing virtue.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:26.300
And courage is a great place
to start, because it's vivid.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:28.370
Everybody knows you
don't want to get killed.

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:30.170
If you can get your
druthers, you're

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:31.730
not going to be tortured.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:35.840
And so the occasions
where that's at risk

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:39.920
are real tests,
real human tests.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.420
You know, Jesus
says "a greater love

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:45.800
hath no man than to give up his
life for his friend," right?

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:49.046
What makes a courageous act?

00:00:49.046 --> 00:00:53.510
STUDENT: The right disposition
towards fear and confidence.

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:56.450
And you have to do it for
the sake of the beautiful.

00:00:56.450 --> 00:01:00.560
LARRY ARNN: So explain
about "right disposition."

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:05.960
Fear and confidence-- why are
fear and confidence opposites?

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.720
STUDENT: Courage is about
finding the mean condition

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.940
between a healthy
awareness of your fear

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:14.360
and your confidence that
allows you to stay and fight

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:17.360
the battle without throwing
away your life unnecessarily

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:19.610
as the rash person does.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:22.250
LARRY ARNN: So all
these virtues--

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:23.930
moral virtues, all
the doing virtues--

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:27.390
have a mean between
two extremes.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.432
And I think Kate
asked a question

00:01:29.432 --> 00:01:31.640
when we talked a long time
yesterday about how do you

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.500
calculate where the middle is?

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.790
And the answer is, first you
identify what the extremes are.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:41.050
And so rashness means what?

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:44.090
STUDENT: Rashness means
an excess of competence

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:46.790
where you aren't
afraid of anything,

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:50.030
and you'll just throw yourself
into the battle whatever

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:50.780
happens.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:54.860
LARRY ARNN: And the
opposite is cowardice.

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:56.720
And we know what
that means, right?

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:00.260
And so you're going to
be in between there.

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:05.300
Would a fully virtuous person
be making that calculation

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.740
in those terms at the time?

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.892
What they be thinking about?

00:02:09.892 --> 00:02:10.600
STUDENT: The end.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:11.750
The goal.

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:14.083
LARRY ARNN: George Washington
at the Battle of Princeton

00:02:14.083 --> 00:02:18.980
is a really great story,
because he had won in Trenton.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:22.790
Christmas morning, they
went across the Delaware

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:25.400
because they'd just been
getting hammered for months.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:29.030
And they needed to win something
in the first year of the war.

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:32.690
And he surprised the Hessians,
and he killed a bunch of them.

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:35.030
And he took Trenton.

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:37.010
But Cornwallis is coming
down with a bigger

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:40.010
force at Princeton
down in New Jersey.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:44.420
And Washington sees that I
can't really be driven out

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:46.100
of here in a single day.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:49.232
That'll deprive us of the
prestige of the victory.

00:02:49.232 --> 00:02:50.690
So he had to go up
there and fight.

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:54.050
And when he got up there, the
American troops were running.

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:57.660
And he walked his
horse through them

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:01.170
and didn't look left or right.

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.510
And he just kept walking
toward the British.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:07.020
And his adjutant records that
Washington couldn't have known

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.440
if anybody had come with him.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:13.470
As he gets close, he
gives the orders to fire.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:16.230
And there's a volley,
and Washington

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:18.870
is shrouded in smoke.

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:22.050
And Fitzwilliam put his
cap down over his face.

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:25.035
He couldn't stand to see
the fallen Washington.

00:03:25.035 --> 00:03:27.690
But then he picked it up,
and the smoke was cleared,

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:29.850
and Washington was still
walking toward the British

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:31.440
on his horse.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:35.320
And the Americans had
formed up behind him.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:38.270
And they ran the British away.

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:40.360
And that's one reason
why George Washington

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:43.070
is the father of our country.

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:49.180
People saw that, and they
thought, I can trust this man.

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:57.040
That is the human being
in action, body and soul.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:01.210
And it's fully realized
in that moment.

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:06.850
And that is the epitome of
moral action, which involves

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:10.510
thinking and decisions, too.

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:14.020
And where's the heart of it?

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:19.300
Where's the thing that makes
it a true virtue to Washington?

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.160
Is it that he didn't get shot?

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:22.870
Is it that they won the battle?

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:25.180
Is it, what?

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:28.780
It's in what he wanted.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:31.750
The motive is all-important.

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:34.250
What do you want?

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:38.420
And what you want,
by the way, is

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:44.510
supremely to do with
practice, especially

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:46.010
through the doing virtues--

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:48.170
but not only.

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:50.630
George Washington,
I will say, he

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:54.300
wrote out these rules
of civil behavior.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:58.400
And so when he was a little boy,
he was practicing all the time.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:03.380
And the rules of civil behavior
include don't bite your nails.

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:05.900
Don't spit when you talk.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:10.760
The last one is labor to
keep alive in your breast

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:14.610
the celestial fire known
as honor, I think he says--

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:17.060
I can't remember [INAUDIBLE],,
memory's failing me.

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:22.070
But that means that he intended
to developed the ability

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:24.450
to do that.

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:30.490
And the way he developed it
was by making good choices when

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:32.980
there's reason not to make them.

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:38.720
I'm Larry Arnn, president
of Hillsdale College.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:41.180
You will find, if you look
at our online courses,

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:43.820
that they cover the whole
range of human learning,

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:47.300
all the high and fundamental
things everyone needs to know.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:49.580
They are free,
because we have always

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:52.550
wished to provide sound
learning to anyone

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:54.880
who wishes to have it.

